  "query_preview": "..An area of low pressure is moving through southern\nArizona and will start to phase into the incoming trough that is\nmoving through the PNW. Moisture from this low pressure system\nwill advect into Utah to produce some low top convection this\nafternoon. Areas across southern and southwestern Utah will have\nsome increased instability this afternoon along with favorable\nshear to sustain some weak thunderstorms. Rain rates with these\nwill be on the order of 0.25-0.5\" per hour, but they will be\nmoving from south to north fairly quickly so residence time will\nbe short-lived making the threat for flash flooding only a concern\nfor areas that experience any training thunderstorms.\n\nThis activity becomes more widespread overnight as the negatively\ntilted trough approaches. This trough will bring a cold front\nthrough the area Saturday morning. Ahead of this front snow levels\nwill be between 8500-9000 feet, but falling to 6000-7000 feet\nbehind the cold front. This cold front will stretch the entire\nlength of the I-15 corridor from southwestern Utah up through the\nIdaho border between 5-8 am. This front will move through quickly\nwith drier air moving in behind it across southwestern Utah.\nCentral and northern Utah will see a bit more post-frontal\nmoisture as a period of northwest flow helps to keep some\nterrain enhanced snow ongoing across the central and northern\nmountains. Additionally, given relatively warm waters across the\nGreat Salt Lake there will be a chance for some lake enhanced snow\nto the south and east of the lake. Most of this activity will wrap\nup by late Saturday afternoon/evening. \n\nQPF totals (25th-75th percentile) will range from 0.25-0.75\"\nacross valley locations with mountains in the 0.50-1.00\" range.\nRelatively higher totals are expected across the southern\nmountains (0.75-1.50\") where winter weather advisories have been\nissued for snow totals of 5-10\" above 9000 feet. Elsewhere snow\ntotals will generally range from 4-8\". Areas that do well in \nnorthwest flow could receive more than 12\", such as the upper \nCottonwoods. If any lake effect snow develops Saturday afternoon \nthen the upper Cottonwoods could get closer to the 90th percentile\nwhich would be 15-18\". \n\nSome of the coldest temperatures of the season will settle into\nthe area Saturday night into Sunday with most locations\napproaching or dipping below freezing, aside from lower Washington\ncounty. A transient ridge will build into the area for Sunday and\nMonday, but the short-lived nature will prevent inversion\nconditions from becoming well established however temperatures\nwill warm back up ~5 degrees above normal.\n\nThe next storm will arrive on Tuesday with colder air associated\nwith it. Median 700 mb temperatures are -8C which could be cold\nenough to bring snow down to valley floors. The latest guidance on\nthis storm exits it a bit more quickly with dry conditions\nfiltering in by Wednesday with some lingering snow across the\nterrain through Wednesday morning. Another storm arrives by\nThursday which will have similarly cold air and even better\nchances for seeing valley snow totals, but ~50% of the guidance\nbrings the storm further south with the best totals across\nsouthern Utah while the other 50% track it from south to north\nwhich would bring similar totals areawide.",
